    "problems": "The study does not test feature splitting, dictionary-size effects, parent-child coverage, or alternative SAE objectives. It reports one SAE architecture and identifies reliance on additive composition as a limitation. Some broader grounding claims are described as qualitative.",
    "rows_left_out": "Omitted output-change rates from Tables 1 and 2, zero-ablation results, random and permutation controls, layer scans, and scale/threshold sensitivity results because they do not test the listed splitting, hierarchy, absorption, canonical-unit, or objective-artifact versions. Table 1 also reports output-change rates separately for the same intervention conditions.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No dictionary-size sweep. No matching of coarse and fine features, and no coverage or direction-proximity test."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "Several VLM families are used, but splitting is never measured in any of them, and only one SAE size is trained."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No nested feature families or concept hierarchy are examined. Pattern and Global sets are task-selectivity groupings."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No parent/child firing or absorption analysis. The co-firing P(G|P)≈100% concerns selected sets, not hierarchical latents."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No cross-size decomposition and no novel-latent analysis."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Single TopK objective. No Matryoshka or other alternative objective is compared."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The relation is downgraded from 'tests' to effectively not testing the claim. Bricken et al. is cited only as background. The text claims Pattern steering helps 'across all splits' and 'across all models', but Table 1 shows InternVL -1.30 pp, which contradicts the statement. The study's 'single SAE architecture' limitation confirms it has no dictionary-size variation."
